1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,280
In about 1 minute, you can import any

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:06,000
location on Earth into Rhino. If you

3
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:07,840
follow this video step-by-step, you will

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,160
learn how to import in Google Maps 3D

5
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:12,440
models with textures directly into your

6
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:14,560
Rhino scene. So, no external software is

7
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:16,200
needed. Everything runs inside Rhino.

8
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:18,360
So, first we need to install the Heron

9
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:20,120
plugin. Open Rhino and type package

10
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:22,040
manager in the command line. A new

11
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:25,520
window will appear. In the search bar,

12
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:27,200
type Heron. Click on it and install the

13
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:30,240
latest version. At the time of recording

14
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:32,119
this video, the latest version is 0.5.0.

15
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:35,720
Click install. Once the installation is

16
00:00:32,119 --> 00:00:39,280
complete, restart Rhino. Now, let's open

17
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:43,840
Grasshopper. Search for the Heron plugin

18
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:45,920
and add uh set anchor point component.

19
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:48,360
Set the toggle to true

20
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:50,080
to enable the Earth anchor point. Here,

21
00:00:48,360 --> 00:00:52,840
we need to define the point of interest.

22
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:55,480
This can be a building name or specific

23
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:57,560
address. In some cases, addresses do not

24
00:00:55,480 --> 00:01:00,480
work properly, so I recommend using

25
00:00:57,560 --> 00:01:02,480
latitude and longitude instead. So, go

26
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:05,000
to Google Maps.

27
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:06,400
Right click on a location, copy the

28
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,680
coordinates,

29
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:11,760
and paste them into a panel in

30
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:16,080
Grasshopper. Then, use the text split

31
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:18,880
component. Use a comma as the separator.

32
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:22,240
Connect the first value to latitude

33
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:24,120
and the second value to longitude. Next,

34
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:26,840
we need a component that allow us to

35
00:01:24,120 --> 00:01:31,040
import 3D model from Google Maps. So, go

36
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:33,920
to GIS API tab and find Google 3D Tiles

37
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:36,200
IOI, which stands for area of interest.

38
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:38,560
In the boundary input, define the size

39
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:41,240
of the area you want to import. Next

40
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:43,880
input is level of detail, which ranges

41
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:46,400
from 0 to 20. I recommend starting

42
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:48,080
around level 13 and increase it

43
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:49,800
gradually once you are happy with the

44
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:52,600
result. You can leave the cache folder

45
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:55,320
as default, and for the API key input,

46
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:57,360
you need a Google 3D Tiles API key. This

47
00:01:55,320 --> 00:02:00,120
step is required to access data from the

48
00:01:57,360 --> 00:02:01,400
Google Maps. To get the API key, go to

49
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:03,720
this link

50
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:06,160
and click start free. Choose your

51
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:07,960
country, fill in your information, and

52
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:10,320
start the free trial. You'll get free

53
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:11,760
access for 90 days. Then, click on

54
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:16,480
console.

55
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:20,160
In the search bar, type map tiles.

56
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:22,560
Open it and click enable.

57
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:26,520
If the key doesn't appear,

58
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:27,800
go to credentials and click show key.

59
00:02:26,520 --> 00:02:30,959
Copy the key

60
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:33,200
and paste it into a panel in Grasshopper

61
00:02:30,959 --> 00:02:35,840
and connect it to the API key input.

62
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:38,360
Finally, set run to true. If you want to

63
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:39,959
preview the model with textures, use

64
00:02:38,360 --> 00:02:41,760
custom preview.

65
00:02:39,959 --> 00:02:44,800
Once you're happy with the result, you

66
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:47,240
can slowly increase the level of detail.

67
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:49,920
Keep in mind that higher levels may take

68
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:51,640
more time to download and import. When

69
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:53,920
everything looks good, bake the custom

70
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:55,320
preview to bring the 3D model into

71
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:58,080
Rhino.

72
00:02:55,320 --> 00:03:00,560
From here, you can render it and further

73
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:02,760
enhance the image using Gemini or

74
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:04,920
ChatGPT. Last but not least, big

75
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:06,760
shout-out to Brian Washburn, the author

76
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:09,800
of Heron plugin, for explaining this

77
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:11,200
workflow on McNeel Discourse forum. If

78
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:13,160
you want to learn more about the newest

79
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:17,120
Grasshopper features available only in

80
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:17,120
Rhino 8, check out this video.

